 The participation rule is a coherent non-Bayesian criterion operating precisely where Bayes is undefined, and it respects every discipline of \cref{sec:commitments}: $\widehat B_i$ quantifies over the agent's own remembered tokens and the groupings it can articulate---``situations like the ones I lost will recur, and a distinction that sorted them would let me act on them''---never over named future representations.
+This is also why the switching hurdle of \cref{ass:commitment} cannot enter the inquiry decision: the policy it would buy lives on a partition the agent does not yet possess, so it cannot be priced before insight---the agent values the question, not the answer.
 The estimate can be wrong, and honestly so: the agent does not know whether a distinction realizing its best grouping exists, which is why $\lambda_i^{(k)}$ prices achievability.
 Two comparative properties organize the applications.
 \emph{Action separation, not information}: the value of a question tracks whether resolving it could change behavior, not how much residual uncertainty remains; an agent for whom no coherent grouping yields positive aggregate robust gain declines to inquire.
@@ -848,11 +849,11 @@ Two comparative properties organize the applications.
 
 \begin{remark}[Enlightened inertia]
 \label{rem:stickiness}
-Because switching is itself costly, acquiring a distinction and acting on it are separate decisions.
-An agent elects inquiry only when the achievability-weighted horizon gain, net of the switching hurdle, covers the inquiry cost (\cref{prop:participation}); but the deliberative leverage $\widehat B_i$ is an estimate, and the refinement that actually arrives may recommend a policy change whose realized horizon gain falls below $\kappa_i$.
-The agent then refines its awareness and updates its model while carrying its old policy forward: it knows the world is not what it thought, yet the change this implies is not worth its cost.
-This is a second source of persistence, distinct from an unpriced question---the model moves and behavior does not---and it feeds the performance differences of \cref{sec:dynamics}.
-Under the pointwise-transparency condition of \cref{rem:certification} the estimate is exact and the two decisions align, so enlightened inertia is a feature of opacity: the very gap the achievability weight prices.
+Acquiring a distinction and acting on it are separate decisions, and they must be, because the policy one would switch to lives on a partition the agent cannot yet represent.
+Pre-insight the agent therefore prices only its question---the stakes $\widehat B_i$ of its present, mis-served record---and cannot fold in the switching hurdle of a refined policy it has no way to name (\cref{prop:participation}).
+Once inquiry delivers a concrete refinement, the switch becomes a well-defined $(S,s)$ decision (\cref{ass:commitment}): the agent may find the realized horizon gain below $\kappa_i$ and carry its old policy forward.
+It then knows the world is not what it thought yet does not act---a second source of persistence, distinct from an unpriced question, and one the agent could not have anticipated, because it could not have anticipated what it would learn.
+This holds whether or not mechanisms are deterministic, and it feeds the performance differences of \cref{sec:dynamics}.
 \end{remark}
 
